## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 64-bit field layouts and perform cross-field moves without branches, a common pattern in graphics and encoding pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masking for 16-bit lanes
- Cross-lane permutation with shifts
- No loops/branches; constant-time operations

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t swapR_B(uint64_t v) {
  uint64_t r = v & 0x000000000000FFFFULL;
  uint64_t g = v & 0x00000000FFFF0000ULL;
  uint64_t b = v & 0x0000FFFF00000000ULL;
  uint64_t a = v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL;
  return (r << 32) | g | (b >> 32) | a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to swap G and A as well? 
- Analyze performance implications on 64-bit architectures with compiler optimizations.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bitwise folding to compute parity without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parity via XOR folding
- Bitwise shifts to mix bits
- No branches or loops in parity path

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t parity32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t p = n;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity32 for 64-bit inputs efficiently?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using a popcount-based method?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-twiddling without branches, a staple in low-level C/C++ interviews.

## Key Concepts
- Parity via XOR folding
- Constant-time bit tricks without loops
- Small lookup for nibble parity

## Code Example
```c
uint32 parity32(uint32 x){
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x &= 0x0F;
  return (0x6996 >> x) & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to handle 64-bit inputs?  
- What are pitfalls with compiler optimizations here?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of in-place bitboard style transforms, pure bitwise logic, and branchless design for a fixed-width data layout.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice permutation across 8x8 matrix
- Masked swaps and shifts to perform transposition in O(1)
- Endianness and layout nullified by a consistent row-major interpretation

## Code Example
```javascript
function transpose8x8(x) {
  // Use BigInt for 64-bit arithmetic in JS
  let t;
  const m1 = 0x5555555555555555n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & m1;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 1n);

  const m2 = 0x3333333333333333n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & m2;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 2n);

  const m4 = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & m4;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 4n);

  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 16x16 on a 128-bit word? needed masks and shifts?
- How would you handle different input mappings (column-major) or endianness concerns?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft fixed, branchless bit-twiddling routines across 128‑bit values, a common requirement in high‑performance bitmap or Bloom filter code. It exposes knowledge of SWAR reductions and careful handling of two 64‑bit lanes.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD within a register) reductions
- Handling 128-bit as two 64-bit halves
- Fixed-mask sequences and non-branching sums

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned hi = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned lo = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type?  - Can you prove correctness with a small formal argument or tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests compact, branch-free parity calculation using classic bit-twiddling techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parity calculation through XOR folding
- Bitwise shifts and XOR operations
- Elimination of loops and conditional branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function parity32(n) {
  n ^= n >>> 16;
  n ^= n >>> 8;
  n ^= n >>> 4;
  n ^= n >>> 2;
  n ^= n >>> 1;
  return n & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this implementation to 64-bit parity calculation?
- Design an approach for computing streaming parity over a byte array?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 32-bit blocks? 
- Can you adapt to compute even parity per byte instead of odd parity?

